markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Flowcharts and Conditionals 

<div style="background-color: #B7E0E5; border-radius: 10px; padding: 15px; margin: 20px auto; max-width: 85%; box-sizing: border-box; word-wrap: break-word; overflow-wrap: break-word;">
<p style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 12px; line-height: 120%;">📖 <strong>Flowchart</strong> a diagram that shows the steps in a process using shapes and arrows. Flowcharts make it easier to plan out and visualize how a program or process works.
</p>
</div>
<br>

## Conditionals in flowcharts

#### Let's take a look at the flowchart below. 

#### This flowchart simulates a simple program that checks the value of the score to determine if the game is won.

<img src="https://images.code.org/3beb270ece0b218a2c2753df93083649-image-1762364855399.png" alt="icon of hand pressing a button" width="350px" style="border-radius:10px; float:left; margin: 0 20px 0px 0px;">


- #### Is `score > 100` is the decision point?
- #### If it’s **true**, the program displays “You win!”
- #### If it’s **false**, it displays “Keep trying!”

<div style="clear:both;"></div>


## From flowchart to code
#### Flowcharts help students see how a program makes decisions **step by step**. 

#### In JavaScript, the logic from the flowchart is written with an **if/else statement**.

<img src="https://images.code.org/160426fe400df8ee9b5a33be77e7380e-image-1762365311358.png" alt="icon of hand pressing a button" width="350px" style="border-radius:10px; float:left; margin: 20px 40px 10px 20px;">

<br>

- #### The decision diamond becomes the **condition** inside the parentheses ()
- #### The `if` block runs if the condition is true, and the `else` block runs if it’s false.

<div style="clear:both;"></div>

---

</div>

<div style="background-color: #B7E0E5; border-radius: 40px; color: #000000; width: 95%; padding: 1px 20px 1px 20px;">

<p style="line-height: 1.5; margin-left: 10px; margin-right: 10px; margin-top: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px; font-size: 18px; ">
<b> 💡Teacher Tip: </b>Each shape in the flowchart connects directly to part of the code — the question becomes the condition, and the arrows become the paths the program can take. Encourage students to trace the code back to the flowchart to check their logic. This helps students see that their code is following the exact plan they created, building confidence and accuracy in their thinking.
</p>

</div>




## What's Next?

#### You will create a flowchart, and the upload the flowchart and work with AI to create an if/else statement. 


MARKDOWN
